---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import ThresholdCurve from "../../components/ThresholdCurve.astro";

const title = "Overfitting — Why Searching Harder Makes It Worse";
const description =
  "Why the best result from a parameter search is mostly luck, how to compute " +
  "the noise floor, and a worked example of +21% in-sample becoming +2% out.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Trading / Overfitting">
  <h1>Overfitting</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Overfitting is finding a rule that describes the past precisely and the
    future not at all. It is not a beginner's mistake — the more sophisticated
    the search, the easier it is to do, because a bigger search finds better
    coincidences.
  </p>

  <h2>The core problem, stated once</h2>
  <p>
    Test one strategy against random data and it probably looks mediocre. Test
    two thousand and the best one looks excellent — <em>by construction</em>,
    because you selected the maximum of two thousand draws from a distribution
    centred on nothing.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The expected best result rises roughly with <span class="c">√(2 ln N)</span>
    as the number of trials N grows. <strong>Searching harder does not find
    better strategies. It raises the bar a real strategy has to clear, and makes
    a fake one easier to believe.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2>You can compute the bar</h2>
  <p>
    Given how many parameter combinations you tried and how much their results
    varied, you can calculate the best Sharpe you would expect from pure noise:
  </p>
  <div class="formula">expected best (no edge) ≈ σ × [ (1−γ)·z(1 − 1/N) + γ·z(1 − 1/(N·e)) ]

N = number of trials,  σ = spread of results,  γ = 0.5772</div>
  <p class="lede">
    A worked instance: 27 combinations with a Sharpe spread of 0.17 gives a noise
    floor of about <strong>0.34</strong>. A best result of 1.19 clears that
    comfortably. Had the same search run over 2,187 combinations, the floor would
    have risen to roughly <strong>0.59</strong> — the identical result becomes
    markedly less convincing purely because more things were tried.
  </p>

  <h2>Seeing it in one picture</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    This is the same phenomenon in its simplest form. As a single threshold
    tightens, the number of surviving stocks collapses. Anywhere on this curve you
    can pick a value that "selects the best stocks" — and the selection is mostly
    an artefact of where you drew the line.
  </p>

  <ThresholdCurve
    field="adx" min={0} max={50} direction="gte" highlight={18}
    title="How many stocks survive as one threshold tightens"
    caption="Real NSE data, recomputed nightly. A rule with three thresholds instead of one has this problem three times over, multiplicatively — which is how a screen ends up with six survivors that share nothing but the fact that they fitted." />

  <h2>The signatures</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>in_sample_gain_that_does_not_transfer</code><span class="meta">the clearest</span></div>
      <p>A real example: re-optimising a strategy's parameters improved Sharpe by
        <strong>+21% in-sample</strong> and <strong>+2% out-of-sample</strong>.
        Almost the entire improvement was selection, not edge.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>a_narrow_peak</code><span class="meta">fragility</span></div>
      <p>If a threshold of 18 scores well while 15 and 21 score badly, that is a
        spike, not an effect. Prefer the centre of a broad plateau even when its
        peak is lower — real relationships degrade smoothly.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>unstable_winners_across_folds</code><span class="meta">easy to check</span></div>
      <p>Refit on rolling windows. If six periods choose six different "optimal"
        parameter sets, the optimiser is fitting noise regardless of how good the
        average looks.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>too_many_conditions</code><span class="meta">rule of thumb</span></div>
      <p>Every extra rule is another chance to fit the past. A strategy with nine
        conditions on a few hundred trades has more parameters than the data can
        support.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What actually helps</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>fewer_trials</code><span class="meta">counterintuitive but true</span></div>
      <p>Two or three parameters on coarse steps. Twenty-seven combinations is
        defensible; two thousand is not, because the noise floor rises with every
        one you add.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>an_economic_reason_first</code><span class="meta">the real defence</span></div>
      <p>Decide <em>why</em> an edge should exist before searching — who is on the
        other side and why they keep losing. A pattern with no explanation is
        usually a coincidence that has not revealed itself yet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>rolling_walk_forward</code><span class="meta">not one split</span></div>
      <p>Fit on everything before a year, trade that year untouched, roll forward.
        Several independent out-of-sample windows rather than one.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>report_the_trial_count</code><span class="meta">honesty test</span></div>
      <p>A backtest quoted without saying how many variants were tried is
        uninterpretable. If nobody mentions it, assume the number is large.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>The uncomfortable part</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    You cannot fully escape this. Every time you look at a result and adjust
    something, you spend a little of your out-of-sample data. By the tenth
    iteration, the "held out" period has quietly informed your choices and stopped
    being a test at all.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The only real protection is genuinely forward data — a strategy tested on
    prices that did not exist when it was designed. That takes months, and it is
    the reason forward paper trading is worth more than any amount of additional
    backtesting.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/backtesting/">backtesting</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/risk-management/">risk management</a> ·
    <a href="/screener/">try it on the screener</a>
  </p>
</Base>
